Rym: This is just a topic that's come up a little bit, both in Marvel Snap and Overwatch.

189
00:15:33,290 --> 00:15:34,212
Rym: So I want to bring up.

190
00:15:34,232 --> 00:15:46,010
Rym: it's been coming up in card games and living games of all kinds for any games, but where there are either characters or cards or things you can get in the game that are like part of the game.

191
00:15:46,090 --> 00:15:59,110
Rym: Like a character you play or a card you play, like a primary mechanic part of a game where new ones come out periodically and players can spend currency to get access to.

192
00:15:59,731 --> 00:16:01,314
Scott: No, it's more broad than that.

193
00:16:01,354 --> 00:16:01,755
Scott: It's any.

194
00:16:01,975 --> 00:16:03,579
Scott: it's any living game, right?

195
00:16:03,739 --> 00:16:06,465
Scott: Any game where the rules change.

196
00:16:06,745 --> 00:16:09,030
Rym: Early magic sets would come out that were just weaker.

197
00:16:09,473 --> 00:16:12,109
Rym: Dark Fallen Empires was just weaker than the previous ones.

198
00:16:13,390 --> 00:16:13,892
Scott: Well, there was.

199
00:16:14,012 --> 00:16:16,359
Scott: there was a little different in that scenario, right?

200
00:16:16,540 --> 00:16:19,750
Scott: They had not realized how they were still figuring it out, right?

201
00:16:20,590 --> 00:16:23,341
Rym: But also, you know, they'll phase out old sets and bring new sets in.

202
00:16:23,381 --> 00:16:25,570
Rym: They have the rotation of what's in the meta right now.

203
00:16:26,310 --> 00:16:26,430
Scott: Right.

204
00:16:26,490 --> 00:16:39,050
Scott: Basically, there was a lesson that was learned in that time that Rym was discussing that when you have a living game where you're, say, adding weapons to an FPS or you're adding characters to a fighting game or you're adding cards to a card game.

205
00:16:39,651 --> 00:16:49,190
Scott: Even if you're not collectible and everyone gets everything right, you have to put anything new that you add to the game.

206
00:16:49,771 --> 00:16:53,256
Scott: There is this if it's even if it's not conscious, right?

207
00:16:53,597 --> 00:17:02,350
Scott: There is this unconscious, like design incentive for the people designing the new parts of that game that are being added.

208
00:17:02,871 --> 00:17:14,910
Scott: Have to be like meaningfully different and probably better than everything that's already included in the game because better is true if you have all players have access to everything.

209
00:17:15,030 --> 00:17:16,813
Rym: I don't think that happens often.

210
00:17:16,973 --> 00:17:17,454
Scott: Absolutely.

211
00:17:17,474 --> 00:17:18,336
Rym: I have not.

212
00:17:18,356 --> 00:17:25,730
Rym: So the Overwatch example I'm going to use all the characters they've added up until now were underpowered at launch and were nerfed over.

213
00:17:26,191 --> 00:17:28,214
Rym: Or were basically buffed over time.

214
00:17:28,233 --> 00:17:36,890
Rym: Now, this is the first time the new character was too powerful and there's a specific change in the monetization model that seems to be tied to this.

215
00:17:37,850 --> 00:17:38,151
Scott: Okay.

216
00:17:38,191 --> 00:17:44,643
Scott: Well, it's happened in many games, even in games where people have everything because most new guns weren't better.

217
00:17:44,663 --> 00:17:45,524
Rym: They just filled a niche.

218
00:17:45,564 --> 00:17:48,530
Rym: The only exception was the P90 and that was a mistake that they admitted to.

219
00:17:48,970 --> 00:18:05,190
Scott: I'm sure that you can think of examples, right, where someone really tried hard not to do this, but there is a very large pattern of games that add things over time, right, where the game designer is you have this in your mind.

220
00:18:05,270 --> 00:18:07,114
Scott: You want people to use the new thing.

221
00:18:07,194 --> 00:18:13,046
Scott: It would suck a lot if you added a new character to the game and everyone was like, fuck that character.

222
00:18:13,086 --> 00:18:14,830
Scott: They suck and they didn't even use it ever.

223
00:18:15,712 --> 00:18:18,210
Scott: Or you added a new weapon to the game and people just kept using the old one.

224
00:18:18,210 --> 00:18:23,970
Rym: So would you believe that happened in Overwatch almost every time they added a new character for a long time?

225
00:18:25,070 --> 00:18:28,750
Scott: Or you added new cards to Magic and no one bought them because the old cards were just stronger.

226
00:18:28,810 --> 00:18:31,139
Rym: I mean, Fallen Empires ain't the best example there because that was, what, 1994?

227
00:18:31,139 --> 00:18:31,320
Rym: Anyway.

228
00:18:34,150 --> 00:18:48,570
Scott: It's happened, but it is a problem in games, I guess, where people, right, it's not like some universal thing like it's going to happen no matter what, but it's this thing you have to sort of consciously avoid doing on purpose.

229
00:18:48,650 --> 00:18:54,760
Rym: I will agree with that, but I think there's a specific inducement that makes this way worse and more sinister.

230
00:18:55,261 --> 00:19:00,530
Rym: It's when people can buy those characters or those items or when there is currency spent.

231
00:19:00,550 --> 00:19:07,650
Scott: It obviously gives you an extra incentive beyond the default of, I want people to use the new thing I worked hard on.

232
00:19:08,351 --> 00:19:09,432
Scott: It's, I need to make money.

233
00:19:09,452 --> 00:19:20,370
Scott: if I allow all Magic cards to be legal and every new set is just worse and not as powerful and the strongest deck is still from the oldest set, I will never sell another Magic card.

234
00:19:21,470 --> 00:19:26,290
Scott: So, yeah, the capitalist incentive obviously enhances this phenomenon.

235
00:19:26,671 --> 00:19:28,254
Rym: So Marvel Snap is a good example.

236
00:19:28,294 --> 00:19:36,270
Rym: Like if they add like, I don't know, dupe to Marvel Snap and it just is a terrible card, like it's worthless, nobody will use it.

237
00:19:36,450 --> 00:19:47,685
Rym: But more importantly, nobody is going to spend in-game currency that translates eventually back out to money to get that card because there's no deck.

238
00:19:47,705 --> 00:19:51,550
Rym: they would put it in so they don't make any money for having even introduced that card.

239
00:19:52,076 --> 00:19:52,886
Rym: Even if the card's okay.

240
00:19:53,870 --> 00:19:55,857
Scott: People have a discussion, right?

241
00:19:56,078 --> 00:19:59,730
Scott: When you buy the Season Pass in Marvel Snap, you get a card, right?

242
00:19:59,990 --> 00:20:08,230
Scott: That otherwise, if you don't buy it then for real money, you have to wait quite a while or use some other currency to be able to get that card.

243
00:20:10,331 --> 00:20:13,439
Scott: So, there's often a discussion of like, is that card good?

244
00:20:14,020 --> 00:20:15,003
Scott: Is it strong enough?

245
00:20:15,123 --> 00:20:16,527
Scott: Is it better than cards I have?

246
00:20:16,607 --> 00:20:17,890
Scott: Am I going to use it in a deck?

247
00:20:18,371 --> 00:20:23,080
Scott: And so therefore, the strength of that card decides whether people will buy it.

248
00:20:23,100 --> 00:20:28,390
Scott: There are people who decide whether or not to buy the Season Pass based on the strength of that card.

249
00:20:28,850 --> 00:20:39,650
Scott: And therefore, whether it's intentional or not, there is a strong incentive for them to make the Season Pass card a card that is at least slightly OP so that people will buy it.

250
00:20:39,770 --> 00:20:56,930
Rym: I've just broadly seen this trend in Marvel Snap where new cards come out, people who prioritize getting those cards earlier, those cards tend to be in the meta of the decks that are winning and eventually get nerfed or modified or counterbalanced in some way further on down the line.

251
00:20:57,730 --> 00:21:00,429
Scott: So that then the new card will be the strongest card.

252
00:21:01,270 --> 00:21:06,390
Rym: So, for the first time, this happened in Overwatch with Mauga, a new character, new tank they added.

253
00:21:06,991 --> 00:21:15,486
Rym: So, basically, the way Overwatch worked before free-to-play was everyone had every character all the time, it's just you have the whole game.

254
00:21:15,526 --> 00:21:17,490
Rym: You paid money, you got the whole game, that's it.

255
00:21:18,431 --> 00:21:22,690
Rym: And they made their money selling loot boxes with like skins and stuff, but no game affecting things.

256
00:21:23,230 --> 00:21:28,850
Rym: In the free-to-play model, when they do introduce a new character, which is rare, they do not introduce them often.

257
00:21:29,251 --> 00:21:38,910
Rym: They add very few characters to this game, but when they do, anyone who bought a Battle Pass basically gets the character right away and everyone else has to wait a few months and then they get the character.

258
00:21:39,390 --> 00:21:40,323
Scott: So, same as Marvel Snap.

259
00:21:40,383 --> 00:21:40,850
Scott: Pretty much.

260
00:21:41,150 --> 00:21:41,731
Rym: Pretty much.

261
00:21:42,312 --> 00:21:50,510
Rym: And now, with this new model, when they introduce Mauga, people are calling it the Battle Pass Diff.

262
00:21:51,550 --> 00:22:03,490
Rym: Because every fucking match, it's not actually a super overpowered character, like you could win, but it's pretty good, especially the way people tend to play the game.

263
00:22:04,431 --> 00:22:10,430
Rym: So, it's winning a ridiculous percentage of matches, like you run into it all the time, or you'll be on a team.

264
00:22:11,390 --> 00:22:13,357
Scott: Everyone, you're just gonna have at least one person.

265
00:22:13,377 --> 00:22:16,790
Scott: The thing is, it's a team game, and you're not gonna have four Amalgas or whatever.

266
00:22:17,250 --> 00:22:18,758
Rym: As long as one person on your team has it, you're good.

267
00:22:20,930 --> 00:22:21,593
Rym: No, no, no, no.

268
00:22:21,793 --> 00:22:24,764
Rym: You have one tank, two DPS, two supports.

269
00:22:24,824 --> 00:22:26,430
Rym: It's like a hockey team, like you have positions.

270
00:22:27,171 --> 00:22:30,660
Rym: So, you only have one tank, and you queue for the role.

271
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:32,484
Rym: I'm playing tank in this game.

272
00:22:32,765 --> 00:22:34,710
Rym: If you join a team, you are the tank.

273
00:22:35,131 --> 00:22:39,429
Rym: If the player who is the tank did not pay for the Battle Pass, they don't have Mauga yet.

274
00:22:41,135 --> 00:22:42,089
Rym: Your team can't have a Mauga.

275
00:22:42,450 --> 00:22:52,904
Scott: So, you either be the Battle Pass and be the Mauga, or if you're not, if you're some other position, and you get teamed with a non-one of those, well, it's like okay.

276
00:22:57,110 --> 00:23:03,806
Rym: So, I've been in matches where some people on the team are just constantly yelling at the tank like, "Why don't you have the Battle Pass?

277
00:23:03,826 --> 00:23:04,969
Rym: You gotta play Mauga or we're gonna

278
00:23:05,029 --> 00:23:05,270
Rym: lose.".

279
00:23:05,270 --> 00:23:06,514
Rym: And they're like really weird about it.

280
00:23:06,995 --> 00:23:12,210
Rym: And Mauga's not even that OP, but he is OP, and they seem to have made it.

281
00:23:12,471 --> 00:23:14,924
Scott: Even if they're a tiny bit OP, it doesn't have to be a lot OP.

282
00:23:15,045 --> 00:23:15,789
Scott: Any amount of OP.

283
00:23:16,310 --> 00:23:20,470
Rym: Even worse, they kind of buffed him right before the holidays and made him even worse.

284
00:23:21,230 --> 00:23:27,170
Scott: Even if everyone on the team are pros, it's like, well, that tiny bit of OP-ness actually would matter even more.

285
00:23:29,814 --> 00:23:40,370
Rym: And it's just, it's bothering me that this is so pervasive in free-to-play games where, to your point, it's easy to do this by accident in a normal situation.

286
00:23:40,610 --> 00:23:55,770
Rym: But in the situation where you're extracting currency from kids with things like Battle Passes, or like the four or five different currencies in Marvel Snap, then yeah, this is just a direct capitalist fucked up incentive.

287
00:23:55,790 --> 00:24:06,609
Scott: This is a great example because people often argue with me when things like League of Legends, and I'm like, "If you don't have every character, then it's still an aspect of

288
00:24:06,629 --> 00:24:07,090
Scott: pay-to-win.".

289
00:24:07,150 --> 00:24:12,729
Scott: And they're like, "No, they think that pay-to-win is only if you had to pay for leveling up or boosting or

290
00:24:12,769 --> 00:24:13,010
Scott: whatever.".

291
00:24:14,273 --> 00:24:17,162
Scott: They're like, "I have the character that I'm gonna use, right?

292
00:24:18,165 --> 00:24:18,626
Scott: It's not

293
00:24:18,686 --> 00:24:19,248
Scott: pay-to-win.".

294
00:24:20,171 --> 00:24:23,218
Scott: I'm not like, you know, I have that character at full power, right?

295
00:24:23,399 --> 00:24:27,730
Scott: And it's like, if it's an asymmetric game and someone has options that you don't have...

296
00:24:28,650 --> 00:24:43,330
Rym: Even if all those options are strictly on a Pareto frontier, it's still unfair because, depending on the composition of your team or whatever you're doing, what if the way the other team's playing, Mauga happens to be the best tank to counter that?

297
00:24:43,510 --> 00:24:47,116
Scott: The game starts before character selection.

298
00:24:47,176 --> 00:24:48,899
Scott: That's the thing people don't understand.

299
00:24:49,300 --> 00:24:52,525
Scott: Before character selection, the game has already begun.

300
00:24:52,625 --> 00:24:54,788
Scott: Choosing the character is a rock, paper, scissors.

301
00:24:54,828 --> 00:24:55,850
Scott: that is part of the game.

302
00:24:56,190 --> 00:25:00,096
Scott: If I have rock and paper, it's like, yeah, they're all equally strong with scissors.

303
00:25:00,677 --> 00:25:08,730
Scott: But if I'm not allowed to pick scissors because I didn't buy it, then I'm sort of at a disadvantage, wouldn't you say, right?

304
00:25:08,810 --> 00:25:13,037
Scott: It's like, sure, I could keep picking rock to beat the people who paid for scissors.

305
00:25:13,358 --> 00:25:14,259
Scott: That can happen.

306
00:25:14,720 --> 00:25:19,088
Scott: But it's still unfair because what am I supposed to do about paper people?

307
00:25:19,168 --> 00:25:20,330
Scott: At best, I can tie them.

308
00:25:21,134 --> 00:25:22,488
Rym: And it's really telling.

309
00:25:22,670 --> 00:25:25,416
Scott: I can pick paper, but then I'll lose.

310
00:25:25,577 --> 00:25:28,383
Scott: if I pick paper to beat paper people, right?

311
00:25:28,724 --> 00:25:31,110
Scott: I'm gonna lose to all the people who paid money for scissors.

312
00:25:31,511 --> 00:25:40,989
Rym: So Overwatch is nerfing him now, like soon, right about when all the people who didn't have a battle pass are gonna get him.

313
00:25:44,531 --> 00:25:49,383
Rym: So, free-to-play is fucking up competitive games in profound ways.

314
00:25:49,423 --> 00:25:52,630
Rym: This is just one tiny but very specific example.

315
00:25:53,130 --> 00:26:07,590
Scott: No, I'm just gonna say, you never could, but you really can't take any game competitively seriously unless every player has the exact same access to everything, basically.

316
00:26:07,670 --> 00:26:14,025
Rym: But look, before Overwatch was free-to-play, they sold skin loot boxes and you paid for the game and it was fine.

317
00:26:14,346 --> 00:26:16,110
Rym: They kind of ruined it by going free-to-play.

318
00:26:16,550 --> 00:26:19,015
Rym: Free-to-play ruins games just across the board.

319
00:26:19,095 --> 00:26:20,178
Rym: It's a bad business.

320
00:26:20,398 --> 00:26:23,765
Rym: Well, it's a bad model for a good game, but it's a great business model.

321
00:26:23,805 --> 00:26:25,970
Rym: Marvel Snap is making fucking boatloads of money.

322
00:26:26,650 --> 00:26:27,890
Scott: Yeah, they just got a big investment.

323
00:26:28,050 --> 00:26:29,332
Rym: Yeah, huge investment.

324
00:26:31,374 --> 00:26:34,638
Rym: But anyway, things of the day.

325
00:26:34,658 --> 00:26:36,560
Rym: It's a little skip.

326
00:26:37,021 --> 00:26:44,830
Rym: So, if you're a fan of Star Wars, then you know that the best character in Star Wars is actually a character who's not in the movies, Vice Admiral Thrawn.

327
00:26:45,651 --> 00:26:47,814
Rym: So, similarly, you play D&D.

328
00:26:48,475 --> 00:26:53,302
Rym: There's a campaign setting called Ravenloft, if you're not up on the D&D world.

329
00:26:53,662 --> 00:26:55,745
Rym: And it's basically Vampire World.

330
00:26:56,046 --> 00:26:58,990
Rym: And it's ruled by Dracula, except he's called Strahd.

331
00:26:59,290 --> 00:27:00,313
Rym: And he's kind of bluish.

332
00:27:00,594 --> 00:27:02,260
Rym: He kind of looks like Thrawn.

333
00:27:02,280 --> 00:27:05,470
Rym: And there's a mystique about him, at least in the old days.

334
00:27:06,171 --> 00:27:07,242
Scott: He's basically just Dracula.

335
00:27:08,310 --> 00:27:10,870
Rym: Yeah, but he's Dracula with blue skin who looks like Thrawn.

336
00:27:12,712 --> 00:27:15,764
Scott: But he's not like the original Dracula.

337
00:27:15,804 --> 00:27:17,350
Scott: He's more like Castlevania Dracula.

338
00:27:17,390 --> 00:27:22,348
Rym: Yeah, he's very much like, "I am basically a god, and what the fuck are you gonna do about

339
00:27:22,388 --> 00:27:22,428
Rym: it?".

340
00:27:23,130 --> 00:27:35,109
Rym: And every D&D... So, he is canonically in the universe of D&D, like forgotten realms, adjacent, because canonically, Ravenloft is connected to all those other campaign worlds.

341
00:27:35,169 --> 00:27:35,690
Rym: for reasons.

342
00:27:37,011 --> 00:27:41,805
Rym: Most people like to run a campaign where you're playing D&D for a while, and then, "Oh shit, we're all trapped in Ravenloft.

343
00:27:41,825 --> 00:27:42,426
Rym: We gotta deal with

344
00:27:42,727 --> 00:27:43,169
Rym: Strahd.".

345
00:27:43,911 --> 00:27:52,090
Rym: But every campaign, any kid actually runs, usually involves you killing Strahd, like fucking him up, even though he's canonically this ultra-powerful thing.

346
00:27:52,770 --> 00:27:58,286
Rym: And this is a very short animation of a little D&D character killing Strahd.

347
00:27:58,647 --> 00:27:59,810
Rym: And it's pretty funny.

348
00:28:01,497 --> 00:28:01,968
Rym: So, what do you got?

349
00:28:05,651 --> 00:28:15,830
Scott: So, there is a... A while ago, a long time ago, I managed to regain access to it, I made a Tumblr of meats from video games.

350
00:28:15,971 --> 00:28:16,808
Rym: Oh yeah, Pixel Meats.

351
00:28:17,430 --> 00:28:18,595
Scott: Pixel Meats, right?

352
00:28:18,655 --> 00:28:22,110
Scott: If you know of any Pixel Meats that are not on my Pixel Meat Tumblr.

353
00:28:22,170 --> 00:28:22,941
Rym: Oh, you're doing it again?

354
00:28:22,961 --> 00:28:23,022
Rym: Ooh.

355
00:28:23,950 --> 00:28:27,010
Scott: I mean, it's been around for, you know, I got access to it a while ago.

356
00:28:27,895 --> 00:28:30,070
Rym: I think there's some new meats I can bring your way.

357
00:28:30,171 --> 00:28:33,090
Scott: I added a handful of meats to it, and then I forgot about it until today.

358
00:28:33,250 --> 00:28:34,639
Rym: I'm gonna link to it from the episode.

359
00:28:35,021 --> 00:28:35,927
Rym: Pixel Meat is back.

360
00:28:35,987 --> 00:28:36,410
Scott: It's gonna be a noose.

361
00:28:36,810 --> 00:28:43,448
Scott: Regardless, somebody made my thing of the day, and their thing of the day is not a Tumblr, it's a standalone website, moei.games.

362
00:28:44,250 --> 00:29:02,969
Scott: And what they have done is, it's sort of obvious when you think about it, 'cause like back in the day, there were just so many video games, which, for absolutely no reason whatsoever, included images of moei statues from Rapa Nui/Easter Island.

363
00:29:04,213 --> 00:29:05,570
Rym: Yep, they were just there.

364
00:29:07,350 --> 00:29:12,450
Scott: Yeah, and they're just like, you play Gradius, and it's like suddenly you're shooting moei heads.

365
00:29:12,910 --> 00:29:17,390
Scott: You play Magic Sword by Capcom, moei heads, right?

366
00:29:17,410 --> 00:29:22,944
Scott: They're just, in a lot of video games, a lot of them, they're just there as an image.

367
00:29:22,984 --> 00:29:25,150
Scott: They're a public domain image, you can just use them.

368
00:29:25,990 --> 00:29:29,409
Scott: I don't know, you know, how anyone would feel about that.

369
00:29:30,732 --> 00:29:34,048
Scott: In terms of, you know, cultural appropriation or anything, I don't know.

370
00:29:36,312 --> 00:29:39,850
Scott: Like, I don't know enough about their history, 'cause I've not studied that.

371
00:29:41,175 --> 00:29:43,670
Scott: I only know things that I've read on the internet.

372
00:29:43,890 --> 00:29:47,170
Rym: Almost no depiction of them in these old games has their hat on either.

373
00:29:48,331 --> 00:29:48,531
Scott: Yeah.

374
00:29:49,333 --> 00:29:57,410
Scott: So, you know, this person catalogued them on a website, much like I catalogued.

375
00:29:58,631 --> 00:30:00,102
Scott: They did a much better job than I did.

376
00:30:00,162 --> 00:30:01,150
Scott: I only got a few meats.

377
00:30:01,972 --> 00:30:03,599
Rym: It's a pretty good Infinite Scroll catalog.

378
00:30:04,081 --> 00:30:05,930
Rym: I wonder, are there more of these or meats?

379
00:30:07,512 --> 00:30:09,684
Scott: There might be more of these than meats.

380
00:30:09,704 --> 00:30:10,709
Scott: There's so many of them.

381
00:30:11,330 --> 00:30:14,030
Rym: I'm scrolling and scrolling, and it keeps loading.

382
00:30:15,173 --> 00:30:16,546
Scott: Oh yeah, there's so many.

383
00:30:20,313 --> 00:30:21,884
Scott: Good job, moai.games.

384
00:30:23,314 --> 00:30:24,269
Scott: In the meta moment!

385
00:30:25,190 --> 00:30:28,150
Rym: The GeekNights Book Club book is The Night is Short, Walk on Girl.

386
00:30:28,370 --> 00:30:32,130
Rym: As made famous in nerd circles by the anime, The Night is Short, Walk on Girl.

387
00:30:33,050 --> 00:30:34,058
Rym: I have started reading it.

388
00:30:34,078 --> 00:30:35,308
Rym: Scott has also started reading it.

389
00:30:35,811 --> 00:30:37,089
Rym: Do you wanna show it at some point?

390
00:30:37,550 --> 00:30:41,269
Rym: The previous book club book, The Murderbot Diaries, I'll just mention because we did a show on it.

391
00:30:42,531 --> 00:30:49,750
Rym: There's such short reads, and they're coming up increasingly in my social circles, frequently.

392
00:30:50,751 --> 00:30:55,850
Rym: Those books are being talked about, so you should probably read The Murderbot Diaries if you listen to GeekNights.

393
00:30:56,150 --> 00:30:56,509
Rym: Just saying.

394
00:30:56,710 --> 00:31:03,110
Scott: Yeah, there might be some internet memeage that you didn't realize was Murderbot jokes until you read the books.

395
00:31:03,330 --> 00:31:04,868
Scott: You can read one book, and that'd be great.

396
00:31:05,050 --> 00:31:05,754
Rym: You'll get most of them.

397
00:31:05,975 --> 00:31:07,764
Rym: Read two books, and you'll get 90% of them, I think.

398
00:31:10,923 --> 00:31:11,128
Rym: So yeah.

399
00:31:12,430 --> 00:31:17,629
Rym: We will be at MAGFest in a couple weeks doing not one, but two panels.

400
00:31:18,710 --> 00:31:20,015
Rym: Those panels are reruns of panels.

401
00:31:20,035 --> 00:31:26,290
Rym: we've done on other cons, but MAGFest is often an environment where-- A couple weeks will be there in like nine days.

402
00:31:27,250 --> 00:31:31,670
Rym: So if you're going to MAGFest, let us know, and we'll probably see you around.

403
00:31:32,111 --> 00:31:36,890
Rym: I plan to spend at least 20% of my time in concerts at this MAGFest.

404
00:31:37,272 --> 00:31:40,609
Rym: At least 10% of my time on DDR machines, assuming they are there.

405
00:31:41,373 --> 00:31:48,029
Scott: And the rest-- I'm not going to budget it, I'm just going to roll with it, but I guarantee I will eat at-- Nando's at least once.

406
00:31:48,890 --> 00:31:51,689
Scott: Nando's chicken, and you know.

407
00:31:52,370 --> 00:31:53,189
Rym: It's going to be a good time.

408
00:31:54,231 --> 00:32:00,770
Rym: I've missed conventions, but MAGFest has always been one of my favorite cons to just go and party at, so I look forward to the party.

409
00:32:02,376 --> 00:32:02,908
Rym: Let's get into it!

410
00:32:03,150 --> 00:32:17,549
Rym: At PEX Unplugged, we played a bunch of tabletop games, and one of them, Scott, comes over and says, "Oh, there's this game, and it looks like it's 18XX-style auctions, but it's not a train game, it's got another thing going

411
00:32:17,689 --> 00:32:17,729
Rym: on.".

412
00:32:18,292 --> 00:32:21,290
Rym: And you kept mentioning it, like, for a while.

413
00:32:21,990 --> 00:32:33,670
Scott: Well, because I went through the first look, and I read the rules to the games that looked like they were worth learning, and weren't going to be big and complicated, or require a bunch of players, or long rules explanations.

414
00:32:34,450 --> 00:32:48,970
Scott: And I saw this one, it was called Whale Street, and the art was kind of good, the production was good, and basically, it wouldn't take us long to learn, because it was mostly the same as 18XX train games.

415
00:32:49,571 --> 00:33:16,210
Scott: And it did something that I had long thought of doing, but few had attempted, which is to make an 18XX game, but to have a simpler, more streamlined operating round, but with that same inside-outside structure, so that you get the same sort of metagame thing, the core, that's the real meat of 18XX in my eyes.

416
00:33:16,491 --> 00:33:24,610
Scott: A lot of people, I think, the core of 18XX for them is the train part, whereas for me, it's the separation of the stock round from the operating round is the core part.

417
00:33:25,130 --> 00:33:35,490
Scott: So, this game replaced the train part entirely with a much simpler game of Texas Hold'em-ish poker.

418
00:33:35,770 --> 00:33:52,941
Rym: Basically, you got companies that will generate poker hands, where some players might know a little more about what that company's up to by having seen a card or two, and you're otherwise playing a pretty straight 18XX stock round

419
00:33:53,483 --> 00:33:54,204
Scott: around

420
00:33:54,224 --> 00:34:02,030
Rym: the poker hands these companies are doing, and the stocks move up or down based on the market of buying and selling, and also based on the poker hands.

421
00:34:02,811 --> 00:34:10,206
Scott: Yep, so instead of companies having this, "Oh, that company's clearly going to be the winner.

422
00:34:10,246 --> 00:34:11,989
Scott: Look how strong it is with capitalism

423
00:34:12,010 --> 00:34:12,170
Scott: snowball."

424
00:34:13,889 --> 00:34:16,476
Scott: It's, "Oh, which company has the best poker

425
00:34:16,496 --> 00:34:16,697
Scott: hand?".

426
00:34:16,717 --> 00:34:21,889
Scott: Well, A, we don't have full information because it's poker and it's not trains where we can see everything.

427
00:34:22,630 --> 00:34:26,909
Rym: And someone runs the company and might make decisions, just like in an 18XX game.

428
00:34:27,291 --> 00:34:32,570
Scott: Someone who's operating a company can choose, just like 18XX, how that company spends its money.

429
00:34:32,670 --> 00:34:45,686
Scott: And now they can choose, "Well, do I spend this company's money to generate a better poker hand to make its stock price go up, or do I spend that company's money on revenues to give me a bunch of money to buy

430
00:34:45,706 --> 00:34:45,826
Scott: others?".

431
00:34:47,090 --> 00:34:50,228
Rym: Or do I just fucking tank the company and sell it all and dump it on someone else?

432
00:34:50,953 --> 00:34:55,150
Scott: Right, it's like there's so many things you can, you know, there's choices to be made there.

433
00:34:56,670 --> 00:35:02,810
Scott: And companies won't snowball so much because your stock price can go back down quite easily.

434
00:35:03,791 --> 00:35:05,877
Scott: And, you know, they have a good hand.

435
00:35:06,058 --> 00:35:09,869
Scott: It's like, "Well, yeah, you had a good hand then, but that doesn't guarantee having a good hand next

436
00:35:09,909 --> 00:35:10,150
Scott: time.".

437
00:35:10,250 --> 00:35:15,948
Scott: Whereas once a company gets its trains going, it's probably going to keep its trains, you know, the companies in 18XX games.

438
00:35:16,690 --> 00:35:18,415
Scott: They get off good, they stay good.

439
00:35:18,877 --> 00:35:20,682
Scott: They get the good tokens, the good tracks.

440
00:35:22,106 --> 00:35:23,370
Scott: You know which ones are going to be good.

441
00:35:23,590 --> 00:35:25,997
Scott: Here, companies can have a bit more up and down.

442
00:35:26,860 --> 00:35:30,170
Scott: It's not a locked-in kind of deal, and that makes it a bit more interesting.

443
00:35:31,030 --> 00:35:34,619
Scott: And so based on that concept, I was like, "We gotta play this game.

444
00:35:34,679 --> 00:35:36,604
Scott: Look, they lock that concept down.

445
00:35:37,426 --> 00:35:39,210
Scott: 18XX but simpler with poker.

446
00:35:39,750 --> 00:35:40,817
Scott: That's a genius thought.

447
00:35:40,837 --> 00:35:42,327
Scott: Whoever thought of it, let's play

448
00:35:42,368 --> 00:35:42,509
Scott: this.".

449
00:35:42,730 --> 00:35:42,870
Rym: Yup.

450
00:35:43,352 --> 00:35:47,462
Rym: And, unfortunately, really, no disrespect to the designers.

451
00:35:47,542 --> 00:35:48,906
Rym: It's well-designed.

452
00:35:48,966 --> 00:35:50,530
Rym: It's got a lot of positive aspects.

453
00:35:51,290 --> 00:36:13,630
Rym: But I'd say over the course of the 30-ish minutes we spent with a group of people who were invested in the concept, learning to play, having a lot of rules clarifications, and then attempting to play, we bounced off of it shockingly hard compared to how enthusiastic we all were coming in.

454
00:36:14,652 --> 00:36:18,450
Rym: This is the first time in a long time I've bounced off of a game unexpectedly.

455
00:36:20,111 --> 00:36:30,580
Scott: Well, I think the hardest, the biggest flaw of the game is that it was not trivial to determine, "Okay, everyone did all their operating.

456
00:36:30,600 --> 00:36:33,770
Scott: They messed with the cards as much as they could.

457
00:36:34,211 --> 00:36:39,048
Scott: We know what the cards are out in the market, the shared cards, because it's a Hold'em style

458
00:36:39,068 --> 00:36:39,410
Scott: poker.".

459
00:36:39,570 --> 00:36:41,803
Rym: We've resolved every rules question.

460
00:36:41,944 --> 00:36:43,070
Rym: Everything is going fine.

461
00:36:43,810 --> 00:36:49,508
Scott: And then the question was, "Okay, which company has the strongest poker

462
00:36:49,548 --> 00:36:49,749
Scott: hand?".

463
00:36:50,412 --> 00:36:53,850
Scott: And answering that question was shockingly difficult.

464
00:36:53,970 --> 00:36:56,389
Rym: Now, when we say shockingly difficult, we messed it up.

465
00:36:56,831 --> 00:37:05,230
Rym: And this is a group of people who I would say are like goddamn experts at the concept of poker hands.

466
00:37:05,510 --> 00:37:05,671
Scott: Right.

467
00:37:05,691 --> 00:37:10,130
Scott: If you're playing poker, it's like actual normal poker, any variety of it.

468
00:37:10,510 --> 00:37:16,330
Scott: You can just reveal your cards and within seconds everyone at the table recognizes that's the winning hand.

469
00:37:16,950 --> 00:37:29,464
Rym: And even if everyone but one person somehow messes it up, like there's 20 people playing Texas Hold'em somehow, at least one person is going to recognize immediately, "Hey, we did this wrong.

470
00:37:29,524 --> 00:37:30,808
Rym: That one wins and it's

471
00:37:30,848 --> 00:37:31,169
Rym: fine.".

472
00:37:31,750 --> 00:37:40,370
Rym: Somehow we messed it up on multiple levels and nobody noticed because everyone was confused.

473
00:37:42,011 --> 00:37:46,170
Rym: And part of this is because the structure of the game is moderately confusing.

474
00:37:46,710 --> 00:37:53,470
Rym: Like there's a lot of confusing steps that really didn't need to be there or didn't seem to serve any game purpose.

475
00:37:54,031 --> 00:38:13,430
Rym: But also instead of using just either a regular deck of cards or a modified but consistent deck of cards, for reasons that I don't really understand, they made their own deck of colors and numbers and also made up their own ranking of what hand is more powerful.

476
00:38:13,530 --> 00:38:18,830
Rym: So if you know poker, you do not necessarily know what makes a stronger hand here.

477
00:38:20,012 --> 00:38:23,025
Scott: So the strongest hand is four of a kind, four of the same number, right?

478
00:38:24,550 --> 00:38:25,660
Scott: So that's easy to spot.

479
00:38:25,740 --> 00:38:26,870
Scott: No one achieved that, right?

480
00:38:27,310 --> 00:38:30,850
Scott: The second strongest hand is like four of the same color, okay?

481
00:38:31,450 --> 00:38:35,650
Scott: And then four numbers in order, so a straight regardless of suit.

482
00:38:36,130 --> 00:38:42,708
Scott: Then three of a kind, three same number, then three same color, then three numbers in order, right?

483
00:38:43,711 --> 00:38:49,430
Scott: And so you go around and you're looking and it's like, aha, I have four numbers in order.

484
00:38:49,650 --> 00:38:53,350
Scott: But wait, I also have four of the same color.

485
00:38:54,695 --> 00:38:57,429
Scott: You have multiples hitting at once because of the shared pool.

486
00:38:58,250 --> 00:39:10,250
Scott: And it's like because it was colors and these numbers and no face cards or anything, it was like, wait, what actually is my hand?

487
00:39:10,350 --> 00:39:15,710
Scott: I thought it was three numbers in order, which I do have, but actually I have three numbers, same color.

488
00:39:15,810 --> 00:39:16,815
Scott: That's what I have.

489
00:39:17,036 --> 00:39:20,210
Scott: And then what does that company have?

490
00:39:21,131 --> 00:39:26,810
Scott: And so it's like, okay, so actually it wasn't the company that we thought that had this.

491
00:39:26,950 --> 00:39:32,790
Scott: It was this other company because they had the same thing, but with a higher number card in there to break the tie.

492
00:39:32,950 --> 00:39:33,822
Scott: We just didn't realize it.

493
00:39:35,072 --> 00:39:47,804
Rym: Couple this with the fact that the rules for when cards flip up, face up so everyone can see them versus remain private, versus when they're in the pool versus when they

494
00:39:47,905 --> 00:39:48,226
Scott: count

495
00:39:48,567 --> 00:39:54,770
Rym: and can be used, we're all just fiddly and you had to keep a lot in your head to figure out the poker hand.

496
00:39:55,250 --> 00:39:59,970
Rym: All the procedures meant that everyone struggled to understand the odds and we messed up the poker hands.

497
00:40:00,592 --> 00:40:03,023
Rym: And that wasn't even the biggest problem.

498
00:40:03,063 --> 00:40:04,690
Rym: That was just the straw that broke the camel's back.

499
00:40:05,170 --> 00:40:10,730
Rym: And suddenly everyone else at the table kind of wilted and all voted to not continue the game.

500
00:40:10,770 --> 00:40:14,330
Rym: And we actually stopped playing after a little more interrogation of the rules.

501
00:40:16,531 --> 00:40:27,390
Scott: I think it's also, it's just, you know, even though the concept was so strong of simplifying the operating round of an 18XX game with something simpler and poker, I don't think was a bad choice.

502
00:40:27,510 --> 00:40:29,036
Rym: Poker's a great choice.

503
00:40:29,417 --> 00:40:32,850
Rym: Poker adjacent, there's a lot of ways, that wasn't even the worst.

504
00:40:33,050 --> 00:40:39,250
Rym: But all the problems of the poker hands were weirdly hard to do, that's not insurmountable.

505
00:40:39,770 --> 00:40:51,490
Rym: The method and means of adding, removing, and revealing cards was not predictable enough for player agency to seem to matter that much.

506
00:40:51,550 --> 00:40:58,110
Rym: Like the value of the companies felt like it was a lot more random than it needed to be.

507
00:40:58,771 --> 00:41:03,402
Scott: Well, I don't think it's how random it was.

508
00:41:03,503 --> 00:41:04,184
Scott: That didn't matter.

509
00:41:04,265 --> 00:41:06,350
Scott: I think what matters is that it was random.

510
00:41:06,470 --> 00:41:07,740
Rym: Well, it was really random.

511
00:41:07,781 --> 00:41:08,950
Rym: It was more like betting a lot of hits.

512
00:41:08,970 --> 00:41:09,734
Scott: But it doesn't matter how random.

513
00:41:09,915 --> 00:41:13,070
Scott: Any randomness at all would have been too much, right?

514
00:41:13,170 --> 00:41:18,346
Scott: We forgot when seeing the concept of, aha, simplified 18XX operating.

515
00:41:18,366 --> 00:41:19,490
Scott: Terrific, right?

516
00:41:19,731 --> 00:41:28,910
Scott: We forget the other core thing of 18XX, which is that there's really no randomness other than who goes first and maybe randomize the private companies to start.

517
00:41:28,930 --> 00:41:35,550
Rym: At the very least, players in the stock round can make reasonable valuation assessments of the companies.

518
00:41:35,910 --> 00:41:43,330
Rym: Here, honestly, it was more like playing an 18XX game, and the companies are effectively betting on three different slot machines.

519
00:41:45,152 --> 00:41:46,697
Rym: And that is not a satisfying game.

520
00:41:46,958 --> 00:41:50,490
Rym: But to make it worse, each company has a built-in power.

521
00:41:50,710 --> 00:41:53,445
Rym: Now, before you think, oh, some 18XX games have that.

522
00:41:53,526 --> 00:41:54,129
Rym: Yeah, they do.

523
00:41:54,910 --> 00:42:07,590
Rym: But these powers were all very different from each other and extremely fiddly to the point that everyone had to keep reading them over and over and over again and then checking to see if they applied or not.

524
00:42:07,790 --> 00:42:11,821
Rym: Like, they were not well-written or designed.

525
00:42:11,881 --> 00:42:15,170
Rym: Like, I would have removed that whole concept or radically simplified it.

526
00:42:16,151 --> 00:42:17,714
Rym: Like, they were just deeply unsatisfying.

527
00:42:17,754 --> 00:42:25,050
Rym: Like, Shell Motors, it has some arbitrary limits and some stuff I don't really want to explain because it's too fiddly.

528
00:42:25,451 --> 00:42:34,850
Rym: But its power is at phase three, if the sum of the tech cards on the market board is 12 or less, this company gets eight revenue.

529
00:42:35,652 --> 00:42:36,273
Rym: So every

530
00:42:36,293 --> 00:42:36,674
Scott: time any

531
00:42:36,774 --> 00:42:49,725
Rym: fucking thing happens in the game, everyone has to look and check, is that true or not, to then determine whether or not this company will have eight more dollars, which will then impact what that company is capable of during its operating round, which determines its valuation.

532
00:42:50,246 --> 00:42:51,389
Rym: Nobody's got time for that.

533
00:42:54,271 --> 00:42:59,920
Scott: But I think you can't have an 18XX simplifying the operating round with some other game.

534
00:43:00,260 --> 00:43:06,470
Scott: that reduces how much time the game takes, reduces complexity, but keeps the core is a great idea.

535
00:43:07,031 --> 00:43:15,850
Scott: But you have to keep the other thing that's true about 18XX, which is that there's really no hidden information and there's really no randomness.

536
00:43:19,211 --> 00:43:30,930
Rym: I would accept a tiny amount of randomness in that I would be okay with the president of a company knowing an X1 card that isn't public right away.

537
00:43:31,750 --> 00:43:39,470
Scott: I mean, yeah, it's obviously more randomness would be more damaging and less would be much better, but zero is really ideal for this kind of game.

538
00:43:40,611 --> 00:43:45,490
Scott: In a real stock market, if someone has an information advantage, they just win.

539
00:43:46,190 --> 00:43:48,894
Rym: The cards in the pool would move too fast.

540
00:43:49,114 --> 00:43:58,988
Rym: Cards would come in quickly and players who ran companies could take actions to push cards through the pool even faster, which added randomness multiplied by randomness.

541
00:44:03,393 --> 00:44:13,430
Rym: And it was really unsatisfying to play and the rules were so fiddly that we spent a long time continually referring to the rules to figure out how to do basic procedural things.

542
00:44:14,170 --> 00:44:23,430
Rym: And then to make matters worse, the physical components, if you so much as bump the table, you will ruin the game because you will not know what anything costs anymore.

543
00:44:24,172 --> 00:44:26,196
Rym: The spaces to put things were too small.

544
00:44:26,216 --> 00:44:32,630
Rym: Lots of little physical design flaws kind of sealed the deal on us bouncing off of this game, unfortunately.

545
00:44:32,830 --> 00:44:39,430
Scott: Yeah, I think they were trying to keep the game small, like physically small, so it wasn't some giant box full of enormous things.

546
00:44:40,350 --> 00:44:45,570
Rym: But there is a reason 18XX games are huge and it is not just because they're train games.

547
00:44:48,391 --> 00:44:51,336
Scott: The smallness saves the money on production.

548
00:44:52,057 --> 00:44:55,944
Scott: B, it makes the game look approachable.

549
00:44:56,024 --> 00:44:57,307
Scott: There isn't that much game here.

550
00:44:57,327 --> 00:44:59,250
Scott: There's only a few types of components.

551
00:45:01,410 --> 00:45:05,238
Rym: There's actually a lot of game here, but it's not the part you think it is.

552
00:45:05,278 --> 00:45:11,590
Rym: It's like an 18XX game, if the operating rounds were not only random, but ten times more complex.

553
00:45:12,652 --> 00:45:16,730
Rym: And then the stock rounds were also more complex, but none of that worked.

554
00:45:17,850 --> 00:45:20,595
Scott: Right, it is simpler than an 18XX game.

555
00:45:21,035 --> 00:45:29,930
Scott: In 18XX, you have to figure out what's the optimal route this company can run with these trains, and that is a harder problem to solve than who has the best poker hand.

556
00:45:29,930 --> 00:45:32,529
Rym: But it's not a confusing problem to solve.

557
00:45:33,671 --> 00:45:38,762
Scott: I mean, it could be, depending on your brain level and how used to it you are.

558
00:45:38,802 --> 00:45:42,630
Scott: The first few times I played, I didn't fucking figure out how to do it properly.

559
00:45:42,710 --> 00:45:56,510
Rym: This game would definitely be confusing, even if you had played it many times, because of the sheer number of random-ish inputs, the iconography instead of just using suits or poker hands, all these factors.

560
00:45:56,610 --> 00:45:59,530
Scott: They should have just used a literal, normal ass deck of cards.

561
00:45:59,670 --> 00:46:02,979
Scott: Or even a deck of cards with some extra suits.

562
00:46:03,560 --> 00:46:07,170
Rym: You could do a poker-type game where there's a fifth suit and it works fine.

563
00:46:07,691 --> 00:46:08,533
Scott: You don't need to, though.

564
00:46:08,593 --> 00:46:09,815
Scott: Just play.

565
00:46:09,936 --> 00:46:11,960
Scott: What's wrong with a 52 deck of cards?

566
00:46:12,942 --> 00:46:14,786
Scott: How would it be better by adding another suit?

567
00:46:14,806 --> 00:46:16,450
Scott: How does that improve it in any way?

568
00:46:16,590 --> 00:46:18,690
Rym: I mean, a bunch of trick-taking games we play add extra suits.

569
00:46:19,390 --> 00:46:24,450
Scott: But I'm saying, for a game of poker, how do you-- Pandante, a poker game, is improved by having another suit.

570
00:46:25,311 --> 00:46:29,570
Scott: Is it improved, or wouldn't it be better to just play Pandante with the regular deck of cards?

571
00:46:29,991 --> 00:46:44,843
Rym: I think Pandante is improved by it, but that's an edge case in fairness, partly because having more potential poker hands-- One, Pandante, even though it has more potential poker hands with the extra suit, the poker rules apply.

572
00:46:44,923 --> 00:46:48,450
Rym: Someone who knows poker logic can apply it to panda logic very easily.

573
00:46:48,570 --> 00:46:50,269
Rym: There's just one extra kind of hand, a flouche.

574
00:46:50,551 --> 00:46:50,965
Scott: The flouche.

575
00:46:51,650 --> 00:46:52,473
